+---
+bump: patch
+type: fixed
+brief: none
+---
+
+A doc-search chunk no longer splits a surrogate pair (`tools/doc-search.mjs`). This closes residue row `2026-10-10-doc-search-chunker-splits-surrogate-pairs`.
+
+`chunk()` cut at a fixed character count. A cut between the two halves of a pair left a lone surrogate at the chunk edge. `JSON.stringify` writes that half as a lone `\udXXX` escape, and no JSON consumer accepts it.
+
+Now a cut that lands inside a pair moves one code unit outward. The chunk keeps the whole character. The loop, the stride and the stop rule do not change. Thus the chunk count does not change, and each cut that was whole stays byte-identical. The cache pairs vector i with chunk i, and that pairing holds.
+
+| corpus (this checkout, `listDocs`) | old `chunk()` | new `chunk()` |
+|---|---|---|
+| files | 1,770 | 1,770 |
+| chunks | 36,538 | 36,538 |
+| chunks with a lone surrogate | 6 | 0 |
+| chunks with different text | — | 6 |
+| files with a different chunk count | — | 0 |
+
+The 6 changed chunks keep their cached vectors, because the body hash and the chunk count still match. Each vector differs from its text by one code unit at an edge. That is the same tolerance that `sanitizeText` in `tools/xerj-load.mjs` accepts. The loader repair stays as a second guard.
+
+`node tools/doc-search.mjs --selftest` plants a pair at each edge of a cut. It also checks that the count and a text with no pair do not change.
